wer-63a00a9b-f175-5563-a7e5-e9656e27a637" "answer-758ee6d2-143b-5d0c-85c7-1b00d67826f9" "answer-aca33ef9-b028-5c98-a62b-f95962819c99" "answer-be2c74de-5ea2-597a-8fc7-7510c00cdf16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974abbd8-6ac1-5b5c-809b-ef8a1790dbd2" :answer/id #uuid "974abbd8-6ac1-5b5c-809b-ef8a1790dbd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "102" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63a00a9b-f175-5563-a7e5-e9656e27a637" :answer/id #uuid "63a00a9b-f175-5563-a7e5-e9656e27a637" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-758ee6d2-143b-5d0c-85c7-1b00d67826f9" :answer/id #uuid "758ee6d2-143b-5d0c-85c7-1b00d67826f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aca33ef9-b028-5c98-a62b-f95962819c99" :answer/id #uuid "aca33ef9-b028-5c98-a62b-f95962819c99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be2c74de-5ea2-597a-8fc7-7510c00cdf16" :answer/id #uuid "be2c74de-5ea2-597a-8fc7-7510c00cdf16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "95" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152296 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" "question-ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" :question/id #uuid "6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326517" :question/problem "A sandwich costs $$12$ before tax. If a $9.5%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $9.5%$ increase to the original cost.\n\nIncreasing by $9.5%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+9.5%=109.5%=1.095\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.095:$\n\n$$\n12\\times 1.095=13.14.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price after tax is $$13.14.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-901b558e-ee47-587e-b837-3417a076e126"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-901b558e-ee47-587e-b837-3417a076e126" :answer-field/id #uuid "901b558e-ee47-587e-b837-3417a076e126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81d65c1e-4fea-5d3d-b067-2a903bca796a" "answer-9a524f5e-5406-5bdc-81e7-caf6bc6d50a9" "answer-fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" "answer-f89dfbd4-8787-51f2-b071-d16433f03c99" "answer-0d2bf66f-071b-599c-870d-beb08d151c52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81d65c1e-4fea-5d3d-b067-2a903bca796a" :answer/id #uuid "81d65c1e-4fea-5d3d-b067-2a903bca796a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$12.75$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a524f5e-5406-5bdc-81e7-caf6bc6d50a9" :answer/id #uuid "9a524f5e-5406-5bdc-81e7-caf6bc6d50a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$12.95$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" :answer/id #uuid "fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$13.14$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f89dfbd4-8787-51f2-b071-d16433f03c99" :answer/id #uuid "f89dfbd4-8787-51f2-b071-d16433f03c99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$13.50$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d2bf66f-071b-599c-870d-beb08d151c52" :answer/id #uuid "0d2bf66f-071b-599c-870d-beb08d151c52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$14.00$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667" :question/id #uuid "ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667" :question/math-academy-id "q-326524" :question/problem "A country welcomed $3,\\,500,\\,000$ tourists in $2018.$  Travel authorities predict that this number will increase by $45%$ by the year $2028.$ Which of the following is closest to the projected number of tourists in $2028?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the projected number of tourists in $2028,$ we apply a $45%$ increase to the $2018$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $45%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+45%=145%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n3,\\,500,\\,000\\times 1.45=5,\\,075,\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of tourists in $2028$ is $5,\\,075,\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa4f452c-1a22-5679-9e96-6dcc369d76d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa4f452c-1a22-5679-9e96-6dcc369d76d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4f452c-1a22-5679-9e96-6dcc369d76d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" :answer-field/choices ["answer-00e18203-4dbf-5257-84e1-5d825b64f9da" "answer-623d64e1-d70f-5584-bac1-b87f99bf2302" "answer-01c47803-fa37-5ad4-a822-f030d4115e5a" "answer-22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" "answer-26239f53-9cc0-56a7-acb2-66f332c11f19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e18203-4dbf-5257-84e1-5d825b64f9da" :answer/id #uuid "00e18203-4dbf-5257-84e1-5d825b64f9da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,\\,000,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-623d64e1-d70f-5584-bac1-b87f99bf2302" :answer/id #uuid "623d64e1-d70f-5584-bac1-b87f99bf2302" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,\\,025,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01c47803-fa37-5ad4-a822-f030d4115e5a" :answer/id #uuid "01c47803-fa37-5ad4-a822-f030d4115e5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,\\,050,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" :answer/id #uuid "22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,\\,075,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26239f53-9cc0-56a7-acb2-66f332c11f19" :answer/id #uuid "26239f53-9cc0-56a7-acb2-66f332c11f19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,\\,100,\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152558 :question/problem "A factory produced $900$ boxes of cereal last year. This year, production is $300%$ greater than last year. How many boxes of cereal did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nboxes of cereal this year." :question/worked-solution "To find this year's cereal production, we apply a $300%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $300%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+300%=400%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $4:$\n\n$$\n900\\times 4=3,600\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $3600$ boxes of cereal this year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149800 :answer-field/correct "answer-6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0" :answer/id #uuid "6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-332f39d2-fe5b-5fe5-bfa7-8da0dfbe5393" :activity/id #uuid "332f39d2-fe5b-5fe5-bfa7-8da0dfbe5393" :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048502 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" "step-aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" "step-af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" "step-ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" "step-00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118"] :activity/first-step "step-ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" :step/id #uuid "ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" :step/content "question-62ed2686-5fd2-570f-9e2c-651f1d63d801" :step/next "step-aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" :step/id #uuid "aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" :step/content "question-aa7ba5f1-091b-5461-b064-b9a572c0acc9" :step/next "step-af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" :step/id #uuid "af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" :step/content "question-6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" :step/next "step-ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" :step/id #uuid "ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" :step/content "question-ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667" :step/next "step-00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118" :step/id #uuid "00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118" :step/content 17592186152558 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0668c811-3c2b-52a1-8452-9d77f52aa1d2" "question-745cf184-0f4c-5376-bafe-668674640edc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0668c811-3c2b-52a1-8452-9d77f52aa1d2" :question/id #uuid "0668c811-3c2b-52a1-8452-9d77f52aa1d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335473" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve is monitoring the population of a newly introduced species of deer. The habitat can sustain a maximum population of $850$ deer, but the herd is still below this limit. Since biologists began tracking the growth, the population reached $490$ deer on month $4$ and $600$ deer on month $12.$ On what month will the population reach $800$ deer? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of deer, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in months.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the farm, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=850$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(850-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{850-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{850-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|850-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $850-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}850-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 850-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 850-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =850-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on month $4,$ the population reached $490$ deer. So, applying the condition $P(4)=490,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =490 \\\\ 850-K{e}^{-4k} & =490 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =360.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on month $12,$ the population was $600$ deer. So, applying the condition $P(12)=600,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =600 \\\\ 850-K{e}^{-12k} & =600 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{360}{250} \\\\ {e}^{8k} & =\\frac{36}{25} \\\\ 8k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{36}{25}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{36}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)} & =360 \\\\ K & =360{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)} \\\\ K & =360\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{25}} \\\\ K & =360\\cdot \\frac{6}{5} \\\\ K & =432\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=850-432{e}^{-(t/8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36/25)}\n$$\n\nFinally, let $m$ be the month when the population reaches $800$ deer. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(m) & =800 \\\\ 850-432{e}^{-km} & =800 \\\\ 50 & =432{e}^{-km} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{50}{432} \\\\ -km & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{216}) \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{216}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $500$ deer on month\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{216})}{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{36}{25})}=47.309…≈47,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dbdf95f2-cd1f-5ed7-bee1-5be1370106ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dbdf95f2-cd1f-5ed7-bee1-5be1370106ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "dbdf95f2-cd1f-5ed7-bee1-5be1370106ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e0c7edc-dd85-5992-a102-fb9214299a63" "answer-2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" "answer-e16e1058-e495-57a8-9aff-1460d83a15f7" "answer-da50aece-8c74-5d03-baea-3fa39526eb88" "answer-ecdba2ac-27cc-530d-9112-686fd6e96177"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e0c7edc-dd85-5992-a102-fb9214299a63" :answer/id #uuid "6e0c7edc-dd85-5992-a102-fb9214299a63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$40\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" :answer/id #uuid "2a9e7d42-5ae6-5bf2-8682-5d96b556c1b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$47\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e16e1058-e495-57a8-9aff-1460d83a15f7" :answer/id #uuid "e16e1058-e495-57a8-9aff-1460d83a15f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$48\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da50aece-8c74-5d03-baea-3fa39526eb88" :answer/id #uuid "da50aece-8c74-5d03-baea-3fa39526eb88" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$56\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecdba2ac-27cc-530d-9112-686fd6e96177" :answer/id #uuid "ecdba2ac-27cc-530d-9112-686fd6e96177" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$60\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-745cf184-0f4c-5376-bafe-668674640edc" :question/id #uuid "745cf184-0f4c-5376-bafe-668674640edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335649" :question/problem "A mountain valley is monitoring the population of a reintroduced elk herd. The valley can sustain a maximum population of $600$ elk, but the herd is still below this limit. Since ecologists began tracking the growth, the population reached $536$ elk on month $4$ and $573$ elk on month $10.$ On what month will the population reach $590$ elk? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population of elk, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in months.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the valley can sustain, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ elk, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(600-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{600-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{600-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on month $4,$ the population was $536$ elk. So, applying the condition $P(4)=536,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =536 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =536 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on month $10,$ the population was $573$ elk. So, applying the condition $P(10)=573,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =573 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-10k} & =573 \\\\ K{e}^{-10k} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-10k}} & =\\frac{64}{27} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{64}{27} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{64}{27}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{64}{27})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} & =64 \\\\ K & =64{e}^{(4/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} \\\\ K & =64{e}^{(2/3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)} \\\\ K & =64{(\\frac{64}{27})}^{2/3} \\\\ K & =\\frac{1\\,024}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=600-\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(64/27)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $m$ be the month when the population reaches $590$ elk. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(m) & =590 \\\\ 600-\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-km} & =590 \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{1\\,024}{9}{e}^{-km} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{90}{1\\,024} \\\\ {e}^{-km} & =\\frac{45}{512} \\\\ -km & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{45}{512}) \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{45}{512}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the population will be $590$ elk on month\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{45}{512})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{64}{27})}=16.905…≈17,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9499f8ab-b442-53e2-8454-60e3c76d0bd5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9499f8ab-b442-53e2-8454-60e3c76d0bd5" :answer-field/id #uuid "9499f8ab-b442-53e2-8454-60e3c76d0bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-74c4a600-f1cd-550e-8301-06f89a755629" "answer-6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" "answer-e38477fa-d2cc-596e-abba-962f5089409f" "answer-9e717efe-369e-59b8-9552-86a39b1b2407" "answer-2c4a4a2d-af4a-591d-a8c6-0601fcd81ab5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74c4a600-f1cd-550e-8301-06f89a755629" :answer/id #uuid "74c4a600-f1cd-550e-8301-06f89a755629" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" :answer/id #uuid "6073c09b-38ea-518e-a68f-3437f1eabe9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$17\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e38477fa-d2cc-596e-abba-962f5089409f" :answer/id #uuid "e38477fa-d2cc-596e-abba-962f5089409f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e717efe-369e-59b8-9552-86a39b1b2407" :answer/id #uuid "9e717efe-369e-59b8-9552-86a39b1b2407" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$22\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c4a4a2d-af4a-591d-a8c6-0601fcd81ab5" :answer/id #uuid "2c4a4a2d-af4a-591d-a8c6-0601fcd81ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207" :question/id #uuid "56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207" :question/math-academy-id "q-344215" :question/problem "A hospital administrator observed that the growth of the number of occupied beds in a hospital is directly proportional to the difference between the hospital’s maximum bed capacity and the current number of occupied beds. Let $H(t)$ be the number of occupied beds, measured in beds, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of beds the hospital can support is $75$ beds, which differential equation could be used to model the number of occupied beds in the hospital? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of occupied beds $H(t)$ in the hospital changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of beds the hospital can support and the current number of occupied beds. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-H),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of beds the hospital can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=75\\text{ beds},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15815c67-c7dd-5da5-a708-0978463c7ec0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15815c67-c7dd-5da5-a708-0978463c7ec0" :answer-field/id #uuid "15815c67-c7dd-5da5-a708-0978463c7ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04278896-0d0b-5abb-8627-033b3e542c42" "answer-6c0c4047-f0a5-5bbe-9e6d-196692c6e874" "answer-5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" "answer-37ece5ef-defd-5270-8c15-ef93957859b1" "answer-09aa1eb1-e4d1-5ba6-831f-ff5b8460e95e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04278896-0d0b-5abb-8627-033b3e542c42" :answer/id #uuid "04278896-0d0b-5abb-8627-033b3e542c42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75+H)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c0c4047-f0a5-5bbe-9e6d-196692c6e874" :answer/id #uuid "6c0c4047-f0a5-5bbe-9e6d-196692c6e874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k(75-H)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/id #uuid "5245158e-ddf4-52ef-a4a6-05b22e789e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k(75-H)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37ece5ef-defd-5270-8c15-ef93957859b1" :answer/id #uuid "37ece5ef-defd-5270-8c15-ef93957859b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH(75-H)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09aa1eb1-e4d1-5ba6-831f-ff5b8460e95e" :answer/id #uuid "09aa1eb1-e4d1-5ba6-831f-ff5b8460e95e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k{H}^{2}(75-H)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-304e96a5-bdbf-59f8-bb8b-043de32c4a6c" :activity/id #uuid "304e96a5-bdbf-59f8-bb8b-043de32c4a6c" :activity/title "Inhibited Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049406 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-7ffb1615-41f0-5c73-b928-54b87da8d950" "step-eea3e168-c7a7-55d1-95df-95b8cbafbc29" "step-1832fe75-dad0-56b1-9a4e-7cace64bb9f3" "step-e1a4f860-833d-5d57-b575-dd51e6470361" "step-07c00b69-3ee6-547e-b310-16e5dd4d50ee"] :activity/first-step "step-7ffb1615-41f0-5c73-b928-54b87da8d950" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7ffb1615-41f0-5c73-b928-54b87da8d950" :step/id #uuid "7ffb1615-41f0-5c73-b928-54b87da8d950" :step/content "question-0668c811-3c2b-52a1-8452-9d77f52aa1d2" :step/next "step-eea3e168-c7a7-55d1-95df-95b8cbafbc29" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eea3e168-c7a7-55d1-95df-95b8cbafbc29" :step/id #uuid "eea3e168-c7a7-55d1-95df-95b8cbafbc29" :step/content 17592186402147 :step/next "step-1832fe75-dad0-56b1-9a4e-7cace64bb9f3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1832fe75-dad0-56b1-9a4e-7cace64bb9f3" :step/id #uuid "1832fe75-dad0-56b1-9a4e-7cace64bb9f3" :step/content "question-745cf184-0f4c-5376-bafe-668674640edc" :step/next "step-e1a4f860-833d-5d57-b575-dd51e6470361" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e1a4f860-833d-5d57-b575-dd51e6470361" :step/id #uuid "e1a4f860-833d-5d57-b575-dd51e6470361" :step/content "question-56f2ee29-f876-5b21-80e6-83404415a207" :step/next "step-07c00b69-3ee6-547e-b310-16e5dd4d50ee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-07c00b69-3ee6-547e-b310-16e5dd4d50ee" :step/id #uuid "07c00b69-3ee6-547e-b310-16e5dd4d50ee" :step/content 17592186317478 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054781 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3986cd63-77a3-5a6f-85dd-edfe43896dec" "question-1c147dd4-dca0-552f-b867-ea163700b916"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054411 :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation $16 + x =10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the left-hand-side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx + 16 = 10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $16$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $16$ from $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 16 &= 10 \\\n    \t\tx + 16 - 16 &= 10 - 16 \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= -6 \\\n\t\tx &= -6\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054782 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-01f80443-1b6b-56dd-8e11-094071306f79" "question-77be8e09-9be9-505d-8aa2-d0adf8a864fa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054412 :question/problem "Solve the equation $x-8 =6.$" :question/worked-solution "To isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $8$ from $x$, which is *adding* $8$ to $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx-8 &= 6 \\\n\t\tx-8+8 &= 6+8 \\\n\t\tx+0 &= 14 \\\n\t\tx &= 14\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054783 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-736d5036-26f1-5b6e-8cfe-36a95468ae26" "question-043ab138-84ef-5c4b-b56a-4e4fc7c053fd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054413 :question/problem "What is the solution to the equation $-15 = p - 12?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\np-12 = -15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $p$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $12$ from $p$, which is *adding* $12$ to $p.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\np-12 \t\t&= -15\\\np-12 + 12 \t&= -15 + 12\\\np+0\t\t\t&=-3\\\np\t\t\t&=-3    \t\t\n}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054784 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-941b508e-aebd-563b-866c-d5adaa00b1e5" "question-31487248-c436-55c5-bd35-253c54a69e52"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054414 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} + z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}+z \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+z & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ to $z$, which is *subtracting* $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ from $z.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z+0 & =\\frac{2}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2-3}{4} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3986cd63-77a3-5a6f-85dd-edfe43896dec" :question/id #uuid "3986cd63-77a3-5a6f-85dd-edfe43896dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-180984" :question/problem "If $9=24+x$, then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n24+x=9\n$$\n\nNow, let's rewrite the left-hand side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx+24=9\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $24$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $24$ from $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & =9 \\\\ x+24-24 & =9-24 \\\\ x+0 & =-15 \\\\ x & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-83710749-ba21-5f55-9039-17b0efe16f24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-83710749-ba21-5f55-9039-17b0efe16f24" :answer-field/id #uuid "83710749-ba21-5f55-9039-17b0efe16f24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6d50a984-cc4d-5057-a6bd-af41905e645d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6d50a984-cc4d-5057-a6bd-af41905e645d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d50a984-cc4d-5057-a6bd-af41905e645d" :answer/id #uuid "6d50a984-cc4d-5057-a6bd-af41905e645d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c147dd4-dca0-552f-b867-ea163700b916" :question/id #uuid "1c147dd4-dca0-552f-b867-ea163700b916" :question/math-academy-id "q-180980" :question/problem "If $10=y+6$, then $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\ny+6=10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $y$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $6$ to $y$, which is *subtracting* $6$ from $y.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =10 \\\\ y+6-6 & =10-6 \\\\ y+0 & =4 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-943d82ef-cd35-58ad-815c-e497dd325956"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-943d82ef-cd35-58ad-815c-e497dd325956" :answer-field/id #uuid "943d82ef-cd35-58ad-815c-e497dd325956" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6b7e41b4-6176-5188-9c37-eacb79e380ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b7e41b4-6176-5188-9c37-eacb79e380ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b7e41b4-6176-5188-9c37-eacb79e380ef" :answer/id #uuid "6b7e41b4-6176-5188-9c37-eacb79e380ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-01f80443-1b6b-56dd-8e11-094071306f79" :question/id #uuid "01f80443-1b6b-56dd-8e11-094071306f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-75381" :question/problem "If $-22+x=11$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the left-hand side so that the first term contains the variable.\n\n$$\nx-22=11\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $22$ from $x$, which is *adding* $22$ to $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-22 & =11 \\\\ x-22+22 & =11+22 \\\\ x+0 & =33 \\\\ x & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad261364-702a-548e-ab79-71e6148c2a9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad261364-702a-548e-ab79-71e6148c2a9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad261364-702a-548e-ab79-71e6148c2a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c305a84e-ea0f-5f3e-896d-f367c6aa26d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fc5fe22-3209-5a7a-b2b2-9e678a15c74d" "answer-395110e5-1076-5070-ba69-53775b81bab0" "answer-d2bda3b0-7850-52d9-ada7-c8caf05b38a6" "answer-1f7a255b-4a91-5596-9df1-aafd7a9adfbb" "answer-c305a84e-ea0f-5f3e-896d-f367c6aa26d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fc5fe22-3209-5a7a-b2b2-9e678a15c74d" :answer/id #uuid "2fc5fe22-3209-5a7a-b2b2-9e678a15c74d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-395110e5-1076-5070-ba69-53775b81bab0" :answer/id #uuid "395110e5-1076-5070-ba69-53775b81bab0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2bda3b0-7850-52d9-ada7-c8caf05b38a6" :answer/id #uuid "d2bda3b0-7850-52d9-ada7-c8caf05b38a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f7a255b-4a91-5596-9df1-aafd7a9adfbb" :answer/id #uuid "1f7a255b-4a91-5596-9df1-aafd7a9adfbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c305a84e-ea0f-5f3e-896d-f367c6aa26d6" :answer/id #uuid "c305a84e-ea0f-5f3e-896d-f367c6aa26d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-77be8e09-9be9-505d-8aa2-d0adf8a864fa" :question/id #uuid "77be8e09-9be9-505d-8aa2-d0adf8a864fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-181006" :question/problem "If $15=x-3$, then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-3=15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $3$ from $x$, which is *adding* $3$ to $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =15 \\\\ x-3+3 & =15+3 \\\\ x+0 & =18 \\\\ x & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39cbddcf-3b52-59f3-b6dc-9e70e1d68663"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39cbddcf-3b52-59f3-b6dc-9e70e1d68663" :answer-field/id #uuid "39cbddcf-3b52-59f3-b6dc-9e70e1d68663" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-28ecdb8e-44c9-59ad-bdfc-c9386de01c31" :answer-field/choices ["answer-28ecdb8e-44c9-59ad-bdfc-c9386de01c31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28ecdb8e-44c9-59ad-bdfc-c9386de01c31" :answer/id #uuid "28ecdb8e-44c9-59ad-bdfc-c9386de01c31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-736d5036-26f1-5b6e-8cfe-36a95468ae26" :question/id #uuid "736d5036-26f1-5b6e-8cfe-36a95468ae26" :question/math-academy-id "q-180989" :question/problem "If $-6+z=-10$, then $z=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the left-hand side so that the first term contains the variable.\n\n$$\nz-6=-10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $6$ from $z$, which is *adding* $6$ to $z.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-6 & =-10 \\\\ z-6+6 & =-10+6 \\\\ z+0 & =-4 \\\\ z & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ecc76ff5-f206-5c3c-8076-84a236b35620"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ecc76ff5-f206-5c3c-8076-84a236b35620" :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc76ff5-f206-5c3c-8076-84a236b35620" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9cac9c78-dacd-5647-8379-091ce37e41aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9cac9c78-dacd-5647-8379-091ce37e41aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cac9c78-dacd-5647-8379-091ce37e41aa" :answer/id #uuid "9cac9c78-dacd-5647-8379-091ce37e41aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-043ab138-84ef-5c4b-b56a-4e4fc7c053fd" :question/id #uuid "043ab138-84ef-5c4b-b56a-4e4fc7c053fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-180993" :question/problem "If $-6=x+10$, then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+10=-6\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $10$ to $x,$ which is *subtracting* $10$ from $x.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+10 & =-6 \\\\ x+10-10 & =-6-10 \\\\ x+0 & =-16 \\\\ x & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04c99036-7c9c-54e0-b3d1-460c9227d8ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04c99036-7c9c-54e0-b3d1-460c9227d8ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "04c99036-7c9c-54e0-b3d1-460c9227d8ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7dd12503-6bd8-5ef9-930a-b3ef8afdfae5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7dd12503-6bd8-5ef9-930a-b3ef8afdfae5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dd12503-6bd8-5ef9-930a-b3ef8afdfae5" :answer/id #uuid "7dd12503-6bd8-5ef9-930a-b3ef8afdfae5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-941b508e-aebd-563b-866c-d5adaa00b1e5" :question/id #uuid "941b508e-aebd-563b-866c-d5adaa00b1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-181042" :question/problem "If $y-3=5.5$, then $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To isolate $y$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $3$ from $y$, which is *adding* $3$ to $y.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =5.5 \\\\ y-3+3 & =5.5+3 \\\\ y+0 & =8.5 \\\\ y & =8.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76a46df6-fe4f-5895-9720-9d0905f45ac5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76a46df6-fe4f-5895-9720-9d0905f45ac5" :answer-field/id #uuid "76a46df6-fe4f-5895-9720-9d0905f45ac5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8fd23cb5-e0b2-55bf-b426-9e17d7324bcd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8fd23cb5-e0b2-55bf-b426-9e17d7324bcd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fd23cb5-e0b2-55bf-b426-9e17d7324bcd" :answer/id #uuid "8fd23cb5-e0b2-55bf-b426-9e17d7324bcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-31487248-c436-55c5-bd35-253c54a69e52" :question/id #uuid "31487248-c436-55c5-bd35-253c54a69e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-101696" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{2}=d-\\frac{5}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & =d-\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ d-\\frac{5}{7} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $d$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $\\frac{5}{7}$ from $d$, which is *adding* $\\frac{5}{7}$ to $d.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}d-\\frac{5}{7} & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ d-\\frac{5}{7}+(\\frac{5}{7}) & =-\\frac{3}{2}+(\\frac{5}{7}) \\\\ d+0 & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ d & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 7+5\\cdot 2}{14} \\\\ d & =-\\frac{11}{14}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d91f9ca7-2eef-5488-b918-4c1430e488c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d91f9ca7-2eef-5488-b918-4c1430e488c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d91f9ca7-2eef-5488-b918-4c1430e488c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8c800a6e-5fa4-5650-a133-c6573f275d8d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c800a6e-5fa4-5650-a133-c6573f275d8d" "answer-7e52f4c8-1fc0-53fc-a21d-5915f65c644a" "answer-2601ce80-d32a-54c8-8faa-4baeaa794638" "answer-0ce9e899-4e9e-5205-b8a7-9b6f65874ee1" "answer-e61a7d4c-1df7-5bb5-a280-59891e321012"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c800a6e-5fa4-5650-a133-c6573f275d8d" :answer/id #uuid "8c800a6e-5fa4-5650-a133-c6573f275d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=-\\frac{11}{14}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e52f4c8-1fc0-53fc-a21d-5915f65c644a" :answer/id #uuid "7e52f4c8-1fc0-53fc-a21d-5915f65c644a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=-\\frac{11}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2601ce80-d32a-54c8-8faa-4baeaa794638" :answer/id #uuid "2601ce80-d32a-54c8-8faa-4baeaa794638" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=-\\frac{13}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ce9e899-4e9e-5205-b8a7-9b6f65874ee1" :answer/id #uuid "0ce9e899-4e9e-5205-b8a7-9b6f65874ee1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\frac{15}{14}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e61a7d4c-1df7-5bb5-a280-59891e321012" :answer/id #uuid "e61a7d4c-1df7-5bb5-a280-59891e321012" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186056026 :tutorial/content "Solving a linear equation involves **isolating** the variable on one side of the equation. \n\nThis can often be accomplished using the **addition principle**. The addition principle tells us that adding (or subtracting) the same number on both sides of an equation does not change its solution.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to solve the equation\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 5.\n$$\n\nThe variable $x$ is not isolated, because we also have a $+4$ on the left-hand side. \n\nTo isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *subtracting* $4$ from $x.$ This way, the $+4$ and $-4$ will cancel out to $0,$ which will leave $x$ alone. Remember to do this to *both* sides of the equation! \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n    \t\tx+4\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} &=5\\,{\\color{red}{-}}\\,{\\color{red}{4}} \\\n\t\tx+(4-4) &=(5-4) \\\n\t\tx + 0 &= 1 \\\n\t\tx &= 1.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is our solution. Let's check that our solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\tx + 4 &= 5 \\\n\t\t1 + 4&= 5 \\\n\t\t5 &=5 \\;{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054917 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120263 :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea1eb590e18a85672f176188f1bab96d85eb1be92d43edd322d1d032500a97ac.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-14t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=7\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=7I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+14I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+14{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-14t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2(-\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2(7\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(7\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha +7\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{50},$ $\\beta =\\frac{21}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{21}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-14t}-\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{21}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{50},\\,\\beta =\\frac{21}{50}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{21}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117722 :answer-field/correct 17592186117725 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d5697b5-d337-5d01-ae32-ec8e66fe10a1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186310974 :answer-field/correct "answer-1f7f5c91-01db-574e-8416-7292a1715248" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f7f5c91-01db-574e-8416-7292a1715248"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f7f5c91-01db-574e-8416-7292a1715248" :answer/id #uuid "1f7f5c91-01db-574e-8416-7292a1715248" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d5697b5-d337-5d01-ae32-ec8e66fe10a1" :question/id #uuid "3d5697b5-d337-5d01-ae32-ec8e66fe10a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336655" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the circuit's current after $0.02$ seconds given that the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.02$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=20I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-5(0)}+\\frac{2}{5} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5(0.02)}+1)≈1.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0bceb32f-2521-5ed4-a561-957b01d5ba94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0bceb32f-2521-5ed4-a561-957b01d5ba94" :answer-field/id #uuid "0bceb32f-2521-5ed4-a561-957b01d5ba94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e608fb46-d69b-55d4-a66f-af2bca92a26f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e608fb46-d69b-55d4-a66f-af2bca92a26f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e608fb46-d69b-55d4-a66f-af2bca92a26f" :answer/id #uuid "e608fb46-d69b-55d4-a66f-af2bca92a26f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.85" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311743 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(12\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 12\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},$ $\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},\\,\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{30}{169}+\\frac{72}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2dbd1490-4230-5bac-885e-7ac22a371c92" :activity/id #uuid "2dbd1490-4230-5bac-885e-7ac22a371c92" :activity/title "Modeling RL Circuits With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051040 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-7b1b56dd-b9e3-5d01-ac4e-820cdf14cd96" "step-cda8dc57-f964-532b-9b39-778d1de7603c" "step-f83669e7-ed4c-5c9e-be92-12862cff41bd" "step-c7e27098-8d0e-5ee5-96b4-0f581c64acfd" "step-a7db3e8f-89ed-5472-b6be-333d89d420fc"] :activity/first-step "step-7b1b56dd-b9e3-5d01-ac4e-820cdf14cd96" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7b1b56dd-b9e3-5d01-ac4e-820cdf14cd96" :step/id #uuid "7b1b56dd-b9e3-5d01-ac4e-820cdf14cd96" :step/content 17592186120263 :step/next "step-cda8dc57-f964-532b-9b39-778d1de7603c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-cda8dc57-f964-532b-9b39-778d1de7603c" :step/id #uuid "cda8dc57-f964-532b-9b39-778d1de7603c" :step/content 17592186311728 :step/next "step-f83669e7-ed4c-5c9e-be92-12862cff41bd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f83669